#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wisp - minimal single-file agent harness, python3 standard library only.

  wisp new --name NAME -m MODEL [-t THINKING] [-s SYSFILE]
           [--env-in-system-prompt] [--parent DIR]   # child sessions: give it always
  wisp run [-d DIR] < prompt
  wisp steer [-d DIR] < message
  wisp continue [-d DIR]
  wisp response [-d DIR]
  wisp skill ls
  wisp skill read SKILL
  wisp agents-md ls
  wisp agents-md read-all
  wisp build-system-prompt [--skills] [--agents-md] [--wisp] < extra
  wisp render-live
  wisp render-past [-d DIR]
  wisp usage [-d DIR]
  wisp ls [--global | --tree [-d DIR]]
  wisp grep [-i] [--global | --tree [-d DIR]] PATTERN

wisp run streams the current turn to stdout as raw `data:` SSE lines and
commits each completed round to <DIR>/wisp.jsonl as it ends. Tool calls and
their results are injected into the same stream as `data:` records, so
the two sources are exclusive and a renderer can follow either one.

wisp steer queues one steering message for a run: the message lands as
one JSON line in <DIR>/wisp.steer.inbox, and a live `wisp run` drains
the inbox at each round boundary and appends each line as a user
record marked wisp_steered, with one `data: {"wisp":"steer"}` line on
the stream. Steering never opens a segment and never resets the round
counter. A run that ends with messages still waiting warns on stderr;
the next `wisp run` injects them before its prompt, so no message is
lost.

wisp render-live opens each thinking block with a `[think]` marker line and
each output block with an `[answer]` marker line, prints each tool-call
header as `[bash 5s 5kb]` with the command on the next line, and prints
each tool result as the same text the model receives: the `wisp:tool`
status header line followed by the raw output.
Tools: bash and python3, 5s default timeout, 5kB default output cap,
spill files under <DIR>/spill/ only when the output overflows the
cap or the command itself exceeds 64kB, never deleted. load_image
puts an image file into the conversation; it resizes to the default
limits unless the call sets resize false.

Run a session from one shell command to the next: take the path from
`wisp new` and export it in one line:

  export WISP_SESSION_DIR=$(wisp new --name NAME -m MODEL)

Give --parent DIR to every child session. A child without --parent
is an orphan. `wisp ls --tree` and `wisp grep --tree` cannot see an
orphan.

WISP_SESSION_DIR binds the session dir: -d DIR wins, then WISP_SESSION_DIR, for
run, continue, steer, render-past, usage and response; without both they fail.
ls and grep bind a session dir only in --tree scope. The value
must be a session dir that holds wisp.jsonl. The value must be exported; a
plain shell assignment does not reach the wisp child process. skill,
agents-md and build-system-prompt take no -d; they scan the CWD walk
and the user-level places.

Env: WISP_SESSION_DIR, WISP_SESSIONS_ROOT, WISP_LLM_URL, WISP_LLM_API_KEY,
WISP_LLM_MAX_TURNS, WISP_LLM_STREAM, WISP_LLM_HEADERS,
WISP_LLM_MODEL_INFO_FILE. WISP_LLM_URL is the full request URL; wisp appends
nothing. WISP_LLM_STREAM=false sends one non-stream chat request per turn
and prints the whole turn as one chunk. WISP_LLM_HEADERS is one JSON object
of extra request headers. WISP_LLM_MODEL_INFO_FILE names a JSON model table
that wisp copies into wisp.modelcfg.
"""

